`loadWeather` used `Promise.all` to await both the main weather API and the ensemble API before rendering. The ensemble API returns a large payload (50+ member arrays × 168 time steps × 3 variables) and can be slow or hang entirely, keeping the page stuck on "Loading weather…" indefinitely. There was also no `AbortController`, so rapid re-submissions stacked concurrent requests.

## Changes

- **Render immediately on main API response** — ensemble fetch is decoupled from the initial render; users see results without waiting for ensemble data
- **Ensemble fetched in background** — hourly charts are silently upgraded with percentile bands when ensemble data arrives; previously selected day is preserved
- **`AbortController` per load** — re-submitting cancels any in-flight request, preventing stale responses from overwriting a newer load
- **Improved error handling** — `AbortError` is silently dropped; genuine ensemble failures surface via `console.warn` instead of being swallowed

```js
// Before — blocks render until both APIs respond
const [response, ensResponse] = await Promise.all([
  fetch(`${WEATHER_API}?${params}`),
  fetch(`${ENSEMBLE_API}?${ensParams}`),
]);

// After — render immediately, upgrade with ensemble when ready
const response = await fetch(`${WEATHER_API}?${params}`, { signal });
// ...render results...
try {
  const ensResponse = await fetch(`${ENSEMBLE_API}?${ensParams}`, { signal });
  // ...update charts with ensemble bands...
} catch (ensError) {
  if (ensError.name !== "AbortError") console.warn("Ensemble data unavailable:", ensError);
}
```

The cat tab had its own separate audio track instead of sharing with photos, and the audio engine was playing all 12 streams (3 themes × 4 tabs) concurrently — causing choppy, unreliable playback especially on mobile.

## Changes

- **Cat → Photos track alias**: `sectionMap` now maps `cat → 'photos'`. Switching between Cat and Photos tabs no longer triggers a track change. The `cats` tab key is removed; `itswal_*_cats.mp3` files are no longer loaded.
- **Single active stream**: `tryAutoplay()` previously called `.play()` on every `Audio` element at once. Now only the current track plays. Crossfade still works — next track fades in while current fades out, then current is `.pause()`d. Max 2 concurrent streams during a crossfade, 1 otherwise.

```js
// Before: 12 streams playing simultaneously (all muted except active)
const players = [];
this.themeKeys.forEach(theme => {
  this.tabKeys.forEach(tab => {
    players.push(this.getTrack(theme, tab).play());
  });
});
await Promise.all(players);

// After: only the active track plays
const track = this.getTrack(this.currentTheme, this.currentTab);
track.volume = this.isMuted ? 0 : 1;
await track.play();
```
